# Local development settings. Copy to `.dev.vars` (gitignored) and fill in.
#
# This file configures your machine and nothing else. `pnpm dev` and
# `pnpm preview` read it; a deployed Worker never does, and no command here
# uploads it. Setting a value on a deployment is a separate act.
#
# All seventeen are set the same way there: each is a plain Worker var. Type it
# on a console fold, read it back or change it on the Worker's Variables and
# Secrets page in the Cloudflare dashboard, or set it from a terminal by
# appending `--var NAME:value` to `pnpm run deploy` — `pnpm run deploy:vars`
# being that last form for a whole file of them. A var arrives with the deploy
# carrying it and not before.
#
# None of them is a Worker secret, and that is deliberate: Cloudflare never
# returns a secret's value, so a console fold could only draw a mask over one
# and an operator could never check what they pasted. Anyone who can open this
# Cloudflare account can read all seventeen, which was already true of the
# account holding them.
#
# An eighteenth name exists and is not below: CONSOLE_TOKEN, the operator
# console's session. It is a secret, and nothing you do sets it — `pnpm run
# console` mints one and writes it to a deployment as a Worker secret,
# it lasts twelve hours, and connecting a second console replaces it, which is
# how a session is revoked. It is absent from this file for the same reason it
# is absent from the checklist the console draws: it is not a value an operator
# supplies. The console keeps its own copy on the operator's machine, in
# `.console/session.json` at `0600` and gitignored, and nowhere else — never
# in this file, so against `pnpm dev` there is no session unless you put a
# value in your own `.dev.vars`. It has to read as a minted session —
# `bg1.<expiry-in-seconds>.<43 or more random characters>` — or it is refused
# as not one
# (packages/operator/src/console/token.ts). DEPLOY.md carries the whole of it,
# including where connecting stands today.
#
# `packages/app/wrangler.jsonc` carries no `vars` block and never will: these
# values are org-specific and half of them are credentials, so committing one
# would put a deployment artifact — and a live key — in a repo meant to be
# forked. It carries `keep_vars: true` instead, which is what stops the next
# `pnpm run deploy` deleting every value you set. DEPLOY.md has the whole of
# it.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD is the only one an operator has to supply. It is left empty on
# purpose, and it is refused when empty with a message naming the variable, so a
# fork that copies this file and deploys cannot end up running on a placeholder
# credential that is identical in every other fork.
#
# The mail and Turnstile values below are fake but valid, so a fresh clone runs
# and signs in without a single account being created anywhere. Neither set
# actually works, and neither is reported as ready.
#
# Turnstile reads Not set up. No donation can complete on the published test
# pair: the widget passes every visitor, and the submission is then refused,
# because Cloudflare's always-passes secret reports the challenge as solved on a
# site your form is not served from. That is the same outage as having no key at
# all, so it is the same word — a milder one is a reading an operator defers.
#
# Mail reads Needs attention, which is the word for a deployment that would send,
# to somewhere that cannot receive: smtp.example.org and donations@example.org
# sit under domains RFC 2606 reserves so that nobody can ever own one, so neither
# can resolve to a mail server or be authorised to send. An arbitrary host that
# will refuse you cannot be told from one that will without dialling it; these
# can. Both say so where they are set.
#
# So nothing this file configures goes green, and that is the point. The
# dashboard is not served until all five set-up jobs read done
# (src/lib/server/config/readiness.ts): signing in lands on the set-up screen
# until then, and on /admin/forms after. Two of the five are rows rather than
# values here — the organisation's registered name and EIN, and the
# notification address — and `scripts/seed-local.sql` writes both into the
# local D1, with the local donation page listed as a site (README.md → the
# quick start). With this file exactly as it ships and that seed applied, one
# job is left: Payments, because neither processor's keys further down are set.
# Uncomment Stripe's with a test pair to reach the dashboard. Where your forms
# may be used is a fold on the console and gates nothing. Anything further down
# is commented out because it has a working default.

# The v0 staff credential, typed at /login with the
# username `admin`. No row stores it and no password hash is ever written —
# sign-in is a timing-safe compare against this value. Changing it is a redeploy, not a database write. There is deliberately
# no in-app change-password or reset flow, and no email: the password is the
# whole credential.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD is the only thing in front of /admin: there is no key
# derivation to make guessing expensive (Workers Free caps CPU at 10 ms per
# invocation, which no usable KDF fits in) and no durable rate limiter. It must
# be at least 12 characters, and longer is the actual defence. Generate one:
# `openssl rand -base64 24`.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=""

# Email. Five values, and the console's mail panel applies them in one press.
#
# The values below are fake and deliberately so. They are syntactically
# valid, so a fresh clone parses them and the app opens with no mail account
# anywhere — but example.org is one of the three domains RFC 2606 reserves for
# documentation, which means nobody can register it and nothing can be delivered
# through it. The checklist recognises both values as the placeholders they are
# and reports mail as Needs attention rather than ready, and a local send fails
# at the socket rather than at the settings. Replace every one but the port to
# send for real.
#
# There is no setting that switches email off, and none should be added. This
# app has one transport, receipts are most of what it is for, and a deployment
# that sends none of them is incomplete rather than configured. Nothing refuses
# to run — donations are taken and the app serves normally — /admin simply says
# so instead of calling it ready.
#
# There is one transport and it is SMTP. Every provider is reached the same
# way — the account's API key or password goes in SMTP_PASSWORD:
#
#   Resend    host smtp.resend.com          user resend      pass re_xxxxxxxx
#   SES       host email-smtp.<region>.amazonaws.com
#                                           user <smtp-user> pass <smtp-password>
#   Mailgun   host smtp.mailgun.org         user postmaster@<your-domain>
#                                                            pass <password>
#   SendGrid  host smtp.sendgrid.net        user apikey      pass SG.xxxxxxxx
#   your own  host mail.example.org         user user        pass password
#
# Nothing in the credential needs escaping. Paste the key or password exactly as
# the provider shows it — `@`, `:`, `/`, `#` and `%` are all ordinary characters
# here. That is exactly why these are four separate variables rather than one
# `smtp://user:pass@host:465` string: with the credential inside a URL,
# Mailgun's `postmaster@domain` login has to be written `postmaster%40domain`
# or the whole value fails to parse. Do not recombine them.
#
# SMTP_PORT is optional and 465 is the only value it may take. 465 is TLS from
# the first byte. 587 is not — it opens in the clear and upgrades only if the
# server offers STARTTLS in a plaintext reply, so anything on the network path
# can remove that offer, and the SMTP client then sends your username and
# password unencrypted and reports the message as delivered. That password is
# usually a live API key. So 587 is refused with a message saying so, as is
# every other port. 25 is refused too, and additionally prohibited outbound by
# Cloudflare itself. If your provider's setup page says 587, use 465 — almost
# all of them offer both.
#
# Postmark cannot be used. It documents that it does not offer port 465 at all,
# only 25/587/2525 with STARTTLS. That is the one provider this rule excludes;
# Resend, Amazon SES, Mailgun, SendGrid and essentially every self-hosted MTA
# offer 465 alongside 587.
#
# A local mail catcher cannot be used. Workers block outbound connections to
# localhost, private-network addresses and Cloudflare's own IP ranges, so
# MailHog/Mailpit on 127.0.0.1 is unreachable from `pnpm dev` and
# `pnpm preview` alike — and this app refuses those addresses by name rather
# than letting them fail as an opaque socket error. Point at a real provider's
# sandbox, or leave the placeholders and accept that local sends fail.
SMTP_HOST="smtp.example.org"
# The one value here you will almost never set at all.
SMTP_PORT="465"
# Half of the credential your provider issued.
SMTP_USERNAME="REPLACE_ME"
# The other half, and usually a live API key.
SMTP_PASSWORD="REPLACE_ME"

# The address donors see in their From line. A bare address
# — not `Name <a@b>` — because it is used as the SMTP envelope sender too.
#
# It keeps its own name rather than becoming SMTP_FROM: it is not a credential
# the provider issued, it is the address a donor reads. It is deploy-time
# rather than a settings row because it is authorised by the same provider that
# issued the credential above — SPF and DKIM are configured on the domain, with
# them — so an address editable independently of the credential is one that
# silently stops delivering. It is not the notification address the console
# sets; that one is where operational mail arrives, and is never donor-facing.
MAIL_FROM="donations@example.org"

# Turnstile — Cloudflare's bot check in front of the public donation form.
#
# Not off by default. The values below are Cloudflare's own documented test keys
# (developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/troubleshooting/testing/): the widget
# renders and passes every visitor, so a fresh clone runs with no Cloudflare
# setup at all. They are convenience, not protection, and /admin says so — it
# recognises these exact keys and reports the form as unprotected rather than
# ready.
#
# For a real deployment, create a widget at dash.cloudflare.com > Turnstile and
# set both. They are minted together and stored together; `pnpm run
# turnstile:keys` prints the pair back as two lines of `.deploy.vars`.
# The sitekey is rendered into every donation form, so it is public wherever the
# form is embedded and there is nothing to keep back.
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY="1x00000000000000000000AA"
# This one never leaves the Worker and the donor's browser never sees it.
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY="1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Nothing below is needed to run the app.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Override for the session-cookie signing key.
#
# Unset — the normal case — auth uses the key in the `auth_signing_key` table,
# minted by `migrations/0002_auth_signing_key.sql` on the first deploy. Nobody
# types it, nobody is shown it, and it is a tamper check over a cookie whose
# authority is the `auth_session` row it names, so a database that a Worker can
# reach is the right home for it.
#
# Set, this wins. That makes it the emergency lever: setting it (or changing it)
# revokes every live session at once without touching the database. Generate one
# with `openssl rand -base64 32`. Rotating the row instead is a one-liner in
# 0002_auth_signing_key.sql's comment.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""

# Optional. Canonical-origin pin.
#
# Unset — the normal case — the origin is derived from each request, so the same
# build answers on http://localhost:5321 (`pnpm dev`), http://localhost:8787
# (`pnpm preview`), your workers.dev subdomain and your custom domain, with no
# value to keep in sync. The origin comes from the request the auth instance is
# built for (src/lib/server/auth/gate.ts), so it is whichever you are running.
#
# Set it only to pin one origin — e.g. a custom domain, where you want
# https://donate.example.org canonical and the workers.dev URL not to be. Note
# that pinning also narrows the trusted-origin list: cookie-bearing HTTP
# mutations (sign-out, revoke-*) are origin-checked by better-auth itself, so
# they then succeed only from this value's origin.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL="https://donate.example.org"

# Stripe — the org's own account. All three, or none: Payments reads Not set
# up until every one of them is set.
#
# Take both keys from the same dashboard screen. They are one pair, and a
# secret key beside a publishable key that came from somewhere else is
# refused by Stripe at the first charge — both are valid on their own and
# nothing in this app checks that they belong together.
#
# STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is not a credential — it is designed to sit in
# public HTML and /api/v1 hands it to any browser that asks for a form's
# config. What goes in the publishable slot must start with pk_: a secret key
# there is published to every visitor, so the checklist reads Needs attention
# and /api/v1 serves nothing until it is replaced.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
# STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# PayPal — the org's own account, and the second processor a deployment may
# be configured with. Either processor's pair finishes the Payments job on its
# own: a deployment holding these two and no Stripe key is set up, and the same
# in reverse (src/lib/server/config/readiness.ts).
#
# PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID is not a credential — PayPal's own script is loaded in the
# donor's page with it in the query string, so it is public wherever a form is
# embedded, exactly as STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is. Both come off the same app in
# PayPal's developer dashboard, and they are one pair.
#
# There is no sandbox. This app reads nothing that says which deployment it is,
# so these are a live PayPal account's values and a gift taken through them is a
# real gift. Rehearsing is a second deployment, as it is for Stripe.
#
# PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID comes back from PayPal rather than being chosen: the
# console's PayPal press registers the listener at this deployment's address
# followed by /api/paypal/webhook, subscribed to SUBSCRIBED_EVENT_TYPES in
# packages/operator/src/paypal/webhook-listener.ts, and stores the id PayPal
# gives it. Without the id, a delivery verifies against nothing and is refused —
# the donor approved a payment that is never captured and never reaches your
# books. A local dev server has no https address PayPal can reach, so this stays
# unset here unless a tunnel's listener id is pasted in by hand.
#
# PAYPAL_CHARITY_RATE_APPROVED is the one value here that is an answer rather
# than a credential: `true` if PayPal has approved this organisation for its
# charity rate, and unset otherwise. PayPal reports on no call which rate an
# account is on, and the rate decides what a donor covering fees is quoted. The
# rates themselves are constants in the tree (src/lib/server/payments/fees.ts) —
# there is no number to type.
# PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID=""
# PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET=""
# PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID=""
# PAYPAL_CHARITY_RATE_APPROVED=""
